.TH USB 4
.SH NAME
usb \- the USB server: the xHCI controller, disks, keyboards, mice, cameras (port "usb")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Ports
.BR usb ,
.BR usbdisk0 ,
.BR usbdisk1 ...,
.BR camera ,
.BR kblight ,
.BR bthci .
A boot module, started by
.BR init (8)
before the disk server, with device rights; with no USB controller it ends
with status 0 and is not started again.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B usb
drives the xHCI controller (found on PCI) and what is plugged in it, hubs
included: each new device is reset, given a slot and an address, its
descriptors read, and each interface handed to its class driver.
.TP
.B disks
USB keys and disks (mass storage, SCSI commands): each is served whole on a
port of its own,
.BR usbdisk N,
with the disk protocol of
.BR vblk (4);
the disk server finds the SieFS partition in it, so a key can be SIEOS's
whole disk.
An unplugged disk's port answers \-EIO.
.TP
.B keyboards and mice
HID devices: their keys, moves and buttons go to the console
.RB ( CON_INJECT ,
.BR con (4)).
.TP
.B cameras
UVC cameras, on the port
.B camera
while one is plugged in: one client at a time; frames come converted to
0x00RRGGBB pixels.
.TP
.B keyboard light
the Legion laptops' ITE 8258 (048d:c195), on the port
.BR kblight .
.TP
.B Bluetooth
an adapter's HCI pipe, port
.BR bthci ,
whose only client is
.BR btd (4).
.SH MESSAGES
On the port
.BR usb :
.TP
.B USB_DISKS
\-> the number of USB disks, once the devices present at start are ready (a
slow key is not waited for more than a few seconds).
.TP
.B USB_DISK
w[1] = index; rbuf <\- usb_disk_t (its port, sector size, present, size in
4 KiB blocks, vendor, product, name). \-> 0.
.PP
On the port
.BR camera :
.B CAM_OPEN
\-> 0, reply w[1], w[2] = width, height (\-EBUSY: another client's);
.B CAM_FRAME
rbuf <\- the next frame (waits up to 1 s) \-> bytes;
.B CAM_CLOSE
stops the camera (it also stops by itself after 3 s without a request).
.PP
On the port
.BR kblight :
.B KBL_SET
w[1] = KBL_OFF, KBL_LOW or KBL_HIGH, w[2] = a colour 0xRRGGBB \-> 0;
.B KBL_GET
\-> the level, reply w[1] = the colour.
.PP
On the port
.BR bthci :
.B BTH_SEND
(w[1] = 0: an HCI command, 1: bulk data; sbuf = the packet),
.B BTH_RECV
(rbuf <\- records {endpoint, 16-bit length, bytes}),
.B BTH_INFO
(\-> vendor << 16 | product).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/kblight
the keyboard light's level and colour, given to the keyboard at each start
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vblk (4),
.BR con (4),
.BR btd (4),
.BR kblight (1)
